  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks to find a step function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap with 1-h under any shift k. The central idea is to use a multi-resolution optimization strategy that combines interpolation from a known parent solution with local optimization. The primary limitations are computational feasibility (1 CPU, 1300 seconds) and the non-convex nature of the problem. We address these by increasing grid resolution dynamically and using efficient interpolation to transfer information between grids. Key hypotheses include:\n   - Increasing resolution improves correlation evaluation accuracy.\n   - Local optimization refines interpolated parent solutions.\n   - Doubling grid size every step balances computational cost with solution quality improvement.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as samples over [0,2]. For grid size n, h \\in \\mathbb{R}^n satisfies:\n- 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\forall i\n- \\sum h[i] = n/2 (assuming n even)\nThe objective is:\n\\[ C(n) = \\max_k \\left| \\sum_i h[i] (1 - h[(i-k) \\mod n]) \\right| \\times \\frac{2}{n} \\]\nwhere the correlation is computed over all lags. For optimization, we use a penalty version:\n\\[ \\Phi(h,\\lambda) = C(n) + \\lambda \\cdot \\left| \\sum h[i] - \\frac{n}{2} \\right|^2 \\]\nwhere \\lambda controls constraint satisfaction. Feasible solutions require projection if constraints are violated.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStep 1: Validate input\n   - Retrieve initial_h_values (n=42)\n   - Ensure even grid sizes for exact sum constraint\nStep 2: Set optimization parameters\n   - lambda_start = 1e4\n   - eval_budget = 50 for n=42, 20 for subsequent grids\nStep 3: For each doubling stage:\n   a. Interpolate parent from previous grid using linear fine-sampling with clamping at boundaries.\n   b. Local optimization with L-BFGS-B for 42 variables, or COBYLA for higher dimensions.\n   c. Penalize constraint violation and bounds.\nStep 4: Update best solution if objective improves.\nStep 5: Early exit if time exceeds budget.\nStep 6: Ensure projection to valid solutions via coordinate-wise adjustment.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Interpolation method: Linear between parent samples, queued at midpoints.\n   - Optimization methods: L-BFGS-B for low dimensions, COBYLA for higher.\n   - Grid sizes: Start at 42, double until time exceeds 40% of budget.\n   - Back-calculate fallback solution if needed.\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness\n   - Stage 1 (42): ~50 evals in ~0.5s\n   - Stage k+1: eval budget decreases exponentially to prevent overflow.\n   - If projection fails, restore best solution from memory.\n   - Handle invalid correlations via padding to pads.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Check 0<=h[i]<=1 and finite values.\n   - Re-evaluate final candidate for exact match to evaluator.\n   - Penalize inadequate memory usage.\n   - Project solutions back to feasibility.",
